\chapter{Markup, document IR, and publishing kernels}
\label{ch:108}
\index{Markup, document IR, and publishing kernels}

Markup and publishing families work with structured representations between input and output. Parsing a format can produce a markup tree, while document IR separates the document model from target-specific rendering. A pretty-printer arranges a supplied document under width and formatting rules. Reuse the representation contract at each boundary to preserve structure, source location and escaping conventions.

\section{Round-trip what the profile promises}
Use a document with nested blocks, literal special characters and a link. Check the intermediate node structure and render it for two admitted targets. Compare semantic structure separately from formatting and check an unsupported node or target explicitly. The rendered text may normalize whitespace and other presentation details. Preserve a separate golden output when the exact publication layout matters. For a generic source formatter, add trivia and syntax-preservation tests as in \cref{ch:50} and \cref{ch:57}; document IR alone does not parse each programming language.

\section{Move a paragraph, then check its spelling on export}
The small DocIR contract test makes a root and a paragraph containing
``Hi \& Bye''. Appending the paragraph transfers it to the root.
Rendering to a typecore buffer and terminating the buffer for a C
string search must yield text containing \OnxCodeTerm{Hi \&amp; Bye}.
The entity spelling belongs to the HTML serialization. Storing the
five characters of the entity in the paragraph in place of its single
ampersand would ask the exporter to escape that ampersand again.
Free the output buffer separately and destroy the root once: the
output is not a view into the paragraph's text.

Extend the exercise by creating a second root. Trying to append the
same attached paragraph there fails and leaves the first tree intact.
To move the paragraph, detach it by index from the first root and
then append that detached node to the second. Between those calls
the caller owns it; after successful append, destruction of the
second root accounts for it. Copying the public node structure by
value would not reproduce these parent relationships. If both trees
must keep independent paragraphs, clone rather than move.

A useful comparison on normalization is two adjacent text siblings
with identical style. In the absence of other distinguishing metadata
they can merge while preserving the text. Give one a source span:
the normalized tree should retain the boundary. Two exports can look
the same to a reader while only one tree retains the provenance needed
to point an editor back to the original bytes.

For an editor test, inspect the normalized nodes as well as the
HTML. A golden file for the displayed paragraph alone cannot
distinguish those two outcomes.

